.Dd August 31, 2026
.Dt BLOCKIO 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm blockio
.Nd display block device activity as a map of the address space
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl a
.Op Fl i Ar interval
.Op Fl color Ar mode
.Op Fl scale Ar scale
.Op Fl thresholds Ar list
.Op Fl decay Ar duration
.Op Fl trail Ar duration
.Op Fl buckets Ar n
.Op Fl half
.Op Fl source Ar backend
.Op Ar device ...
.Nm
.Fl l
.Nm
.Fl demo Ar n
.Nm
.Fl once
.Op Fl for Ar duration
.Op Fl size Ar WxH
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
draws what the disks are doing as a map: one pane per device, one cell per
slice of the device's address space, green for reads, red for writes and
amber for TRIM.
Cell zero is LBA zero in the top left, and cells run left to right and top
to bottom, so a whole device fits a pane whatever its size.
A resilver or a scrub appears as a bright band sweeping across the map.
.Pp
.Nm
must run as root whatever it reads.
On
.Fx
the data comes from DTrace's
.Xr dtrace 1
.Va io
provider, so the DTrace modules must be loaded:
.Pp
.Dl kldload dtraceall
.Pp
and device geometry comes from
.Xr diskinfo 8 .
On macOS the data comes from
.Xr fs_usage 1
and the geometry from
.Xr diskutil 8 ,
neither of which needs anything turned off; see
.Sx MACOS .
.Pp
Named devices are watched, with or without a
.Pa /dev/
prefix.
Given no names and no
.Fl a ,
.Nm
offers a list of every whole disk, all of them preselected but the disk
images; devices without media are left out.
The list is
.Va kern.disks
filtered by what answers
.Xr diskinfo 8
on
.Fx ,
and the whole disks
.Xr diskutil 8
reports on macOS.
.Pp
The options are:
.Bl -tag -width "-thresholds list"
.It Fl a
Watch every disk, without asking.
.It Fl l
List the disks and exit.
.It Fl i Ar interval
Sampling interval.
The default is 100ms.
.It Fl color Ar mode
Color depth:
.Cm auto
(the default),
.Cm truecolor ,
.Cm 256 ,
.Cm 16
or
.Cm off .
Truecolor gives the decay a continuous gradient, 256 colors a five step
ramp per command, 16 colors dim and bright, and
.Cm off
draws the trail as a middle dot and activity as block characters of
rising density.
.Ev NO_COLOR
is honored.
.It Fl scale Ar scale
How a byte rate becomes a color.
.Cm auto ,
the default, measures each device against its own busiest cell, so a quiet
disk still shows structure.
.Cm fixed
measures against
.Fl thresholds ,
so the same color always means the same rate.
.It Fl thresholds Ar list
Hot and cold steps for
.Cm fixed ,
in bytes per second
.Em per cell ,
ascending, comma separated, one to eight of them.
.Cm K , M
and
.Cm G
suffixes are binary.
The default is
.Ql 64K,512K,4M,32M .
A cell covers a slice of a device rather than all of it, so useful
thresholds are far below the device's total throughput.
.It Fl decay Ar duration
Half-life of activity on the map.
The default is 500ms.
.It Fl trail Ar duration
Half-life of the trail, the dim record of where the head has been.
The default is 60s;
.Ql 0
turns it off.
.It Fl buckets Ar n
How finely the kernel splits each device.
DTrace aggregates each I/O into
.Ql offset * buckets / mediasize ,
so this is the ceiling on how fine the map can be.
Zero, the default, asks for about as many as the terminal can draw.
The value is clamped to 256 through 16384 and fixed for the run: enlarging
the window afterwards does not make the map finer.
.It Fl half
Draw two rows of cells per terminal row, the top half of each cell in the
foreground color and the bottom half in the background one.
On by default; it needs a background color, so it is ignored with
.Fl color Cm off .
.It Fl source Ar backend
Where the I/O comes from:
.Cm auto ,
.Cm dtrace ,
or
.Cm fsusage .
.Cm auto
is the only backend on
.Fx
and
.Cm fsusage
on macOS.
.It Fl demo Ar n
Fabricate
.Ar n
devices and traffic instead of tracing anything.
Needs neither root nor disks, and is the easy way to look at the layouts and
the color modes.
.It Fl once
Sample for a while, print one frame to standard output and exit, for
scripting or for pasting into a report.
.It Fl for Ar duration
How long
.Fl once
samples.
The default is 2s.
.It Fl size Ar WxH
Frame size for
.Fl once .
The default is 100x30.
.El
.Ss The display
Every cell carries two layers.
Activity fades with the half-life of
.Fl decay ,
so the bright part of the map is what the disk is doing now.
Under it a dimmer trail fades with the half-life of
.Fl trail ,
showing where the head has been.
.Pp
The pane header names the device, its size and its description, and the
line below gives read, write and TRIM rates in bytes and operations per
second, with the total moved since the map was last cleared.
.Pp
Panes go in one column for one or two devices and two columns above that, so
three devices sit in the four pane layout, five in the six pane layout and
seven in the eight pane layout.
.Ss Keys
.Bl -tag -width "1 to 9" -compact
.It Ic space , p
Pause.
The map freezes;
.Xr dtrace 1
keeps running.
.It Ic c
Clear the map and the totals.
.It Ic s
Switch the color scale between
.Cm auto
and
.Cm fixed .
.It Ic + , -
Double or halve the thresholds, switching to
.Cm fixed .
.It Ic 1 No to Ic 9
Show one device full screen; the same key returns.
.It Ic 0 , a
Show every device again.
.It Ic q , esc , ^C
Quit.
.El
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width BLOCKIO_CONFIG
.It Ev BLOCKIO_CONFIG
Path of the configuration file, overriding the default.
.It Ev XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Base directory of the configuration file.
.It Ev NO_COLOR
If set, the map is drawn without color.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "~/.config/blockio/config"
.It Pa ~/.config/blockio/config
Defaults, one
.Ql option = value
per line, with
.Ql #
comments.
The options are
.Cm color , scale , thresholds , decay , trail , buckets , half
and
.Cm interval ,
each taking the same values as the flag of that name.
Flags override the file, and a bad line is a warning rather than an error.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Watch both halves of a mirror while it resilvers, with colors that mean the
same thing on both:
.Pp
.Dl "blockio -scale fixed -thresholds 1M,8M,32M,128M ada0 ada1"
.Pp
Follow a scrub for a long time, keeping the trail so the swept region stays
visible:
.Pp
.Dl "blockio -a -trail 10m"
.Pp
Capture one frame for a report:
.Pp
.Dl "blockio -a -once -for 5s -size 120x40 > frame.txt"
.Pp
Look at the eight pane layout on a machine with two disks:
.Pp
.Dl "blockio -demo 8 -color truecolor"
.Sh MACOS
System Integrity Protection hides every kernel probe, so
.Va io:::start
matches nothing until SIP is relaxed from recoveryOS.
The default backend is therefore
.Xr fs_usage 1 ,
which reports the kdebug events the buffer cache raises for every completed
I/O: a block number in the whole disk's address space and a byte count,
which is all the map needs.
.Pp
An APFS volume's I/O is reported against the synthesized container it lives
in, never the physical disk underneath, so
.Pa disk0
sits dark while
.Pa disk3
does the work.
.Fl l
lists both.
.Pp
macOS synthesizes a device for every APFS container and two for every
mounted disk image, so one SSD and a handful of Xcode simulator runtimes
come to fourteen entries.
The images are listed but start unselected in the picker;
.Fl a
still watches every one of them.
.Pp
Neither backend sees TRIM: unmap does not travel through the buffer cache,
so the amber layer stays empty.
.Pp
.Fl source Cm dtrace
is for a machine that has had SIP relaxed with
.Pp
.Dl csrutil enable --without dtrace
.Pp
from recoveryOS, which on Apple silicon wants the security policy lowered to
Reduced Security first.
macOS carries the Solaris
.Va io
provider, so it is a different D program:
.Va args Ns [0]
is a translated
.Vt bufinfo_t ,
the direction is
.Va b_flags No & Dv B_READ ,
the size is
.Va b_bcount
and the position is
.Va b_blkno .
Devices are keyed by minor number, because the
.Vt devinfo_t
translator in
.Pa /usr/lib/dtrace/io.d
leaves
.Va dev_name
and
.Va dev_statname
as the literal
.Qq ?? .
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr dtrace 1 ,
.Xr fs_usage 1 ,
.Xr systat 1 ,
.Xr diskinfo 8 ,
.Xr diskutil 8 ,
.Xr gstat 8 ,
.Xr iostat 8 ,
.Xr zpool 8
.Pp
The scripts shipped beside
.Nm ,
.Pa iosnoop.d
and
.Pa iosum.d ,
show the same data as text.
.Sh CAVEATS
On
.Fx ,
each logical I/O reaches the
.Va io
provider twice, once at the GEOM layer and once at the disk layer.
.Nm
traces the disk layer only, so nothing is counted twice, but activity to a
provider that never reaches a disk, such as a fully cached read, is not
shown at all: this is a map of what the disks do, not of what the file
system was asked for.
.Pp
The bucket count is fixed at startup, baked into the generated D script or
into the accumulator that folds
.Xr fs_usage 1
lines into frames.
Resizing the terminal rearranges the panes but does not make the map finer;
restart for that.
